WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.210
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.075
Building Global Secure Access.

3
00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:11.737
This is Chapter 1: the decision,
licensing, and availability in GCC.

4
00:00:11.737 --> 00:00:15.793
A network control is adopted by a
written decision with exit criteria,

5
00:00:15.793 --> 00:00:18.589
not by turning on a toggle,
so the first code reads

6
00:00:18.589 --> 00:00:21.875
what the tenant holds and
checks the decision against it.

7
00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:26.068
We'll start from what Microsoft Learn
says, see what it leaves to you,

8
00:00:26.068 --> 00:00:31.100
read the kit's call list, its availability
table and nine checks, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.419
One note before we begin: this
chapter is a draft awaiting review,

10
00:00:35.419 --> 00:00:39.440
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.652
Why start with a decision?

12
00:00:44.652 --> 00:00:48.676
Because each function of Global
Secure Access has its own gate:

13
00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:52.081
the Microsoft traffic profile
comes with Entra ID P1,

14
00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:56.414
while Internet Access and Private
Access are licensed separately.

15
00:00:56.414 --> 00:00:58.065
So the goal is concrete.

16
00:00:58.065 --> 00:01:02.426
By the end you can read the tenant's
licenses and onboarding state with the

17
00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:04.285
least-privileged permissions,

18
00:01:04.285 --> 00:01:07.573
keep one decision record for
each of the four functions,

19
00:01:07.573 --> 00:01:11.005
and show an assessor that each
record is current, licensed,

20
00:01:11.005 --> 00:01:14.365
approved, and honest about what
GCC does and doesn't state.

21
00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:17.985
It's for the developers, the policy
owner who signs the records,

22
00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:20.690
and the security officer who keeps them.

23
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.315
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

24
00:01:26.315 --> 00:01:31.752
Learn's known limitations page says
Global Secure Access is available in GCC,

25
00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:36.255
for the product as a whole; no
feature page narrows or widens that,

26
00:01:36.255 --> 00:01:40.503
so a preview feature's GCC
availability is not yet specified.

27
00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:45.849
Learn names the licenses: the Microsoft
traffic profile with Entra ID P1 or P2,

28
00:01:45.849 --> 00:01:51.278
and Internet Access and Private Access
separately, or through the Entra Suite.

29
00:01:51.278 --> 00:01:55.383
Two Graph reads tell you what the
tenant holds: the subscribed SKUs,

30
00:01:55.383 --> 00:02:00.087
with License Assignment Read All, and
the tenant status on the beta endpoint,

31
00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:02.327
with Network Access Policy Read All.

32
00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:06.450
Both live on the global
service, which GCC uses.

33
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.563
What does Learn leave to you?

34
00:02:11.563 --> 00:02:14.202
One GCC sentence covers the whole product,

35
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:18.576
so nothing says anything per feature,
and preview features are silent.

36
00:02:18.576 --> 00:02:22.055
License names come without
service plan identifiers,

37
00:02:22.055 --> 00:02:24.375
so a script can't match a license by ID.

38
00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:29.325
The onboarding read is beta, and
beta APIs are subject to change.

39
00:02:29.325 --> 00:02:31.588
And nothing covers the decision itself.

40
00:02:31.588 --> 00:02:34.073
So the kit adds an availability table,

41
00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:38.883
one row per feature with an interim
path where GCC isn't stated; a decision

42
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:44.013
record that names the SKU the agency reads
from its own export; and nine checks.

43
00:02:44.013 --> 00:02:48.660
The verdict: keep Learn's statements
as they are, and build what it leaves.

44
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.760
This is where GCC matters.

45
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.545
Because Learn's sentence
covers the product,

46
00:02:57.545 --> 00:03:01.459
every preview feature's GCC
availability is not yet specified,

47
00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:05.072
and the kit's table lists them
with the guide's interim path.

48
00:03:05.072 --> 00:03:09.211
Registered devices: use joined
or hybrid joined devices.

49
00:03:09.211 --> 00:03:11.303
The cloud firewall for clients:

50
00:03:11.303 --> 00:03:15.247
block QUIC with an Intune-delivered
Windows Firewall rule.

51
00:03:15.247 --> 00:03:19.072
The Explicit Forward Proxy:
acquire with the client only.

52
00:03:19.072 --> 00:03:23.210
HTTP method filtering:
filter by category and name.

53
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:27.010
Custom Private Access
profiles: per-app applications.

54
00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.810
Enriched log categories: not
exported without a decision.

55
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.497
A decision that uses one without
an approved exception fails,

56
00:03:34.497 --> 00:03:37.180
and the finding says what to do instead.

57
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:45.643
Here are the nine checks Test-Gsa-Decision
runs on the collection and the records.

58
00:03:45.643 --> 00:03:47.243
The tenant is onboarded.

59
00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:50.079
There's one record for each
of the four functions.

60
00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:53.968
Each availability quote was read
on Learn within thirty days.

61
00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:58.193
An adopted function has enough licenses
for the pilot and the first ring.

62
00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:03.342
Each record has an approver, exit
criteria and a review date within a year.

63
00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:07.329
A deferred or retained function
names what protects it meanwhile.

64
00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:10.459
And any preview feature
has an approved exception,

65
00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:12.768
or the finding names the interim path.

66
00:04:12.768 --> 00:04:17.160
The checks only read files;
the policy owner still signs.

67
00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.010
Who is involved?

68
00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:26.773
The policy owner signs each decision
record and approves any exception.

69
00:04:26.773 --> 00:04:29.285
Developers build and maintain the kit.

70
00:04:29.285 --> 00:04:34.210
The security officer keeps the records,
the license export and the run folders.

71
00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:39.886
And the reader holds very little: License
Assignment Read All for the licenses,

72
00:04:39.886 --> 00:04:43.117
on version 1.0, and
Network Access Policy Read

73
00:04:43.117 --> 00:04:46.610
All for the tenant status,
on beta, delegated only.

74
00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.710
Global Reader is enough for both.

75
00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:53.905
And since November 18, the guide's step
connects with these same two permissions;

76
00:04:53.905 --> 00:04:59.150
before, it used Organization Read All,
a higher permission Learn also lists.

77
00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:09.045
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder, against one

78
00:05:09.045 --> 00:05:14.655
fictional tenant, contoso dot example,
seen during its pilot and two weeks later.

79
00:05:14.655 --> 00:05:15.867
No tenant at all.

80
00:05:15.867 --> 00:05:21.198
Step 1 lists what the decision area reads:
two calls, each with its least-privileged

81
00:05:21.198 --> 00:05:25.667
permission, one of them beta, both
verified against their Learn pages.

82
00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:30.325
Step 2 lists the features whose GCC
availability Learn doesn't state,

83
00:05:30.325 --> 00:05:33.756
each with the interim path
the kit expects instead.

84
00:05:33.756 --> 00:05:37.540
The first rows are on the slide;
the chapter shows them all.

85
00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:45.452
Step 3 reads the pilot tenant's
licenses and onboarding state.

86
00:05:45.452 --> 00:05:49.690
Step 4 checks the four decision
records, and finds four failures.

87
00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.740
The internet decision's availability
quote is forty-six days old.

88
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.903
Its SKU has twenty-five
units for sixty users.

89
00:05:56.903 --> 00:06:01.852
It uses the cloud firewall for clients,
a preview feature, with no exception.

90
00:06:01.852 --> 00:06:04.548
And the branch decision
defers remote networks

91
00:06:04.548 --> 00:06:07.378
without naming what protects
branches meanwhile.

92
00:06:07.378 --> 00:06:10.302
Step 5 reads the preview finding in full.

93
00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:13.364
Look at the fix: it isn't
"enable the preview".

94
00:06:13.364 --> 00:06:18.640
It's the guide's interim path, a Windows
Firewall rule delivered by Intune.

95
00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:25.540
Step 6 checks the same
records two weeks later.

96
00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:29.828
The internet record was re-read on
Learn and drops the preview feature,

97
00:06:29.828 --> 00:06:31.897
its SKU now has seventy-five units,

98
00:06:31.897 --> 00:06:36.629
and the branch record names its
compensating controls: branch traffic stays

99
00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:40.990
on the current TIC path, and managed
devices at branches keep the client.

100
00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.377
Every check passes.

101
00:06:42.377 --> 00:06:47.028
Step 7 runs this chapter's tests:
thirteen passed, none failed.

102
00:06:47.028 --> 00:06:49.230
Then delete the output folder.

103
00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.153
Lab 2 moves to a test
tenant, never production,

104
00:06:56.153 --> 00:07:00.955
and a tenant with no Global Secure
Access licenses is a valid result.

105
00:07:00.955 --> 00:07:04.743
Sign in as a person with the two
least-privileged permissions.

106
00:07:04.743 --> 00:07:07.329
Run the guide's own
reads as a cross-check.

107
00:07:07.329 --> 00:07:11.764
Then collect and check with the kit,
using your own decision records,

108
00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:15.168
kept on agency storage and
never in this repository.

109
00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:18.068
The kit's license counts
should match the guide's.

110
00:07:18.068 --> 00:07:22.417
If the tenant isn't onboarded,
onboard it before any later chapter.

111
00:07:22.417 --> 00:07:25.009
The lab only reads, so backing it out is

112
00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:28.268
signing out and filing
or deleting the outputs.

113
00:07:28.268 --> 00:07:30.620
The authors haven't run this lab.

114
00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:35.820
What does the output prove?

115
00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.304
The license and onboarding reads, hashed,

116
00:07:38.304 --> 00:07:42.833
and the decision records support the
system security plan and the control

117
00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:47.582
on external system services; the decision
supports information exchange too.

118
00:07:47.582 --> 00:07:51.390
The guide maps no key security
indicator to a decision record,

119
00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.182
and the kit doesn't invent one.

120
00:07:53.182 --> 00:07:56.995
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

121
00:07:56.995 --> 00:08:01.794
The dangers: adopting a preview
because the product is in GCC, a stale

122
00:08:01.794 --> 00:08:07.108
availability quote, and beta reads in
an unattended job, each with its guard.

123
00:08:07.108 --> 00:08:09.828
You're done when every
function has a current,

124
00:08:09.828 --> 00:08:13.283
signed record, and no preview
runs without its exception.

125
00:08:13.283 --> 00:08:18.020
Next, Chapter 2: the configuration
export, and logs first.
